<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leonid Kravchuk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1934">1934</a>–<a href="/years/2022">2022</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leonid Kravchuk was a Ukrainian politician who rose through the Communist Party of the Ukrainian SSR and became chairman of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verkhovna-rada">Verkhovna Rada</a> in 1990. He oversaw the declaration of independence on 24 August 1991 and was elected the first president of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> in December 1991. He signed the Belovezha Accords dissolving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and served until 1994.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leonid-kravchuk.jpg" alt="Leonid Kravchuk" width="308" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yuriy Ivanov / Юрий Иванов · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leonid_Kravchuk_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leonid Makarovych Kravchuk was born on 10 January 1934 in the village of Velykyi Zhytyn in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a>, into an ethnic Ukrainian peasant family. The village was then part of the Second Polish Republic and was annexed into the Ukrainian Soviet Socialist Republic after the Soviet invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1939. His parents worked under Polish landowners, and his father, who had served in the Polish Army, was killed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Kravchuk joined the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> in 1958 and worked in the party apparatus in Chernivtsi Oblast from 1960. He entered the party's Central Committee in 1970 and became head of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Agitation and Propaganda in 1980, where he promoted ideological propaganda and fought what the party called Ukrainian bourgeois nationalism. He became second secretary on 23 June 1990. Between 1989 and 1991 he changed from a hardline propagandist against Ukrainian nationalism into a supporter of it, a shift that the scholar Alexander Motyl described as a metamorphosis.</p><p>He became Chairman of the Supreme Soviet of the Ukrainian SSR in 1990, opposed the coup attempt of 1991 and led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> to independence. A referendum in December 1991 confirmed it, and he was elected president. He was inaugurated on 5 December 1991, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radaa">Rada</a> also renounced the 1922 treaty creating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Kravchuk said at the ceremony that the empire which had endured for 337 years no longer existed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>As president, Kravchuk relied on an informal coalition of national communists and moderate national democrats. He treated state-building as more important than economic reform, supported the creation of an independent Armed Forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, gave up Ukraine's nuclear arsenal and welcomed the enlargement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>. Economic reform was postponed, and gross domestic product contracted by 40% during his time in office.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> miners in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donbas">Donbas</a> began striking on 2 September 1992. Kravchuk dissolved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verkhovna-rada">Verkhovna Rada</a> on 17 June 1993 and called snap parliamentary and presidential elections for 1994. He lost the presidency on 19 July 1994 to his former prime minister Leonid Kuchma. He then served as a People's Deputy from 1994 to 2006 and died on 10 May 2022, aged 88, the last surviving signatory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belovezha-accords">Belovezha Accords</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leonid Kravchuk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 16 July 1990 the Supreme Soviet adopted the Declaration of State Sovereignty, and <b>Leonid Kravchuk</b> became its head.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ukrainian-ssr">Ukrainian SSR · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The song was sung as the de facto anthem at the inauguration of <b>Leonid Kravchuk</b>, the first president of independent Ukraine, on 5 December 1991.</blockquote><a href="/songs/national-anthem-of-ukraine">State Anthem of Ukraine</a></li><li><blockquote>Meeting in the Belovezha Forest, Boris Yeltsin, <b>Leonid Kravchuk</b> and Stanislav Shushkevich declared that the Soviet Union had ceased to exist and created the Commonwealth of Independent States.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-8">8 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Velykyi Zhytyn, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.66,26.35&amp;year=1991" aria-label="Velykyi Zhytyn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5151 405 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Leonid Kravchuk"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5601 675h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5601 675h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.66,26.35&amp;year=1991">What was here in 1991? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
